<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Harvey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1578">1578</a>–<a href="/years/1657">1657</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Harvey was an English physician, the first known to describe completely and in detail the circulation of the blood, pumped by the heart through the lungs and around the body. Taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> by Fabricius, he served St Bartholomew's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1609 and was physician to James I and Charles I. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-motu-cordis">De Motu Cordis</a>, published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1628, combined experiments with a calculation: if <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> were right, the liver would have to make 498 pounds of blood a day. The theory took twenty years to win general acceptance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-harvey.jpg" alt="William Harvey" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Daniël Mijtens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Harvey_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harvey was born on 1 April 1578, the eldest of nine children of Thomas Harvey, a jurat of Folkestone who served as its mayor in 1600, and Joan Halke. He was educated at Folkestone and the King's School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, and entered Gonville and Caius College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1593, taking his Bachelor of Arts in 1597. He then travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and entered the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1599, where he came to know Fabricius and read his work on the valves of the veins. He graduated Doctor of Medicine at Padua on 25 April 1602.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Harvey took a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> doctorate in the same year and became a fellow of his college. He joined the Royal College of Physicians on 5 October 1604, married Elizabeth Browne a few weeks later, and was elected a Fellow of the College in 1607. As Lumleian lecturer from 4 August 1615 he began lectures on anatomy in April 1616, intending to cut up as much as possible before the audience and to illustrate man by the structure of animals. In 1618 he became Physician Extraordinary to James I.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-motu-cordis">De Motu Cordis</a> appeared in 1628 he is said to have fallen mightily in his practice because of the criticism of other physicians, but he was re-elected Censor of the College in 1629 and later became its treasurer. From 1632 he served Charles I as Physician in Ordinary, and the king's hunts gave him many deer to examine. He was also a sceptic about witchcraft. Sent to examine a woman accused in 1632, he dissected her toad and found it ordinary, and his report on four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> women in 1634 led to their acquittal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-motu-cordis">De Motu Cordis</a>, a quarto of 72 pages in 17 chapters, was printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, where an annual book fair would spread it quickly. Harvey wrote that the heart's movement was so rapid that he was almost tempted to think it could be understood only by God. With only a small lens he had to rely on reasoning in places. Before him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> held that blood passed between the ventricles through invisible pores, and Ibn al-Nafis had described blood moving from the heart to the lungs and back. Some doctors said they would rather err with Galen than proclaim the truth with Harvey.</p><p>His On Animal Generation (1651) began with the development of the hen's egg. It stated that all life comes from the egg, which was the first definite statement against spontaneous generation. Harvey retired from public life after the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1646 and died at Roehampton on 3 June 1657, probably of a cerebral haemorrhage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Harvey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Science spoke Latin for two more centuries: Copernicus’s On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres of 1543, <b>William Harvey</b>’s account of the circulation of the blood, Newton’s Principia of 1687 and the works in which Carl Linnaeus named plants and animals with two Latin words were all written…</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1628 <b>William Harvey</b>, physician to Charles I of England, showed by experiment and calculation that the blood circulates, pumped by the heart.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Folkestone, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Roehampton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.08,1.18&amp;year=1629" aria-label="Folkestone on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4577 393 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Harvey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5027 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5027 663h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.08,1.18&amp;year=1629">What was here in 1629? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I profess both to learn and to teach anatomy, not from books but from dissections; not from the positions of philosophers but from the fabric of nature…&#8221;</p><footer>On the Motion of the Heart and Blood (1628), dedication to the College of Physicians &#183; tr. Robert Willis, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67065/pg67065-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The heart, consequently, is the beginning of life; the sun of the microcosm, even as the sun in his turn might well be designated the heart of the world…&#8221;</p><footer>On the Motion of the Heart and Blood (1628), chapter 8 &#183; tr. Robert Willis, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67065/pg67065-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I found the task so truly arduous, so full of difficulties, that I was almost tempted to think, with Fracastorius, that the motion of the heart was only to be comprehended by God.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Motion of the Heart and Blood (1628), chapter 1 &#183; tr. Robert Willis, 1847 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/67065/pg67065-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
